 "findings": [
  {
   "#": "F1",
   "Evidence": "Fig. 1",
   "Observation": "Access correlates with log GDP at \u03c1 = 0.97 between countries, 0.60 within countries and 0.06 for year-to-year changes.",
   "Interpretation": "Wealth and electrification are linked through long-run structural differences between countries, not short-run growth; a GDP boom year should not be expected to raise access. Shared time trends inflate within-country correlations."
  },
  {
   "#": "F2",
   "Evidence": "Fig. 1, 3",
   "Observation": "The renewables share is negatively associated with access (\u03c1 = -0.78 pooled); South Africa has the lowest share (10%) and the highest access.",
   "Interpretation": "WDI's renewable share includes traditional biomass, so a high share mainly signals reliance on firewood and charcoal in poorer, rural countries, not clean electricity. It should not be read as 'renewables reduce access'."
  },
  {
   "#": "F3",
   "Evidence": "Fig. 2",
   "Observation": "Urbanisation and access rise together (\u03c1 = 0.88 between countries); the most urban countries have the highest access in both 2000 and 2024.",
   "Interpretation": "Density lowers the cost per grid connection, which is consistent with the pattern, but urban measurement bias (surveys reach towns more easily) and wealth could explain part of it."
  },
  {
   "#": "F4",
   "Evidence": "Fig. 5",
   "Observation": "Tanzania (break 2011: 0.4 \u2192 2.9 pp/yr), Zambia (2014: 0.9 \u2192 2.4) and Zimbabwe (2015: 0.2 \u2192 2.4) show structural acceleration; South Africa decelerated after 2005 (1.9 \u2192 0.4).",
   "Interpretation": "The timing coincides with the spread of off-grid solar and rural-electrification programmes in the 2010s, but the data contain no programme variables; methodological changes in the access estimates could also produce a kink. South Africa's slowdown is consistent with approaching saturation."
  },
  {
   "#": "F5",
   "Evidence": "Fig. 4",
   "Observation": "Smoothing Zambia's series turns 2007 (raw 18.5%) into 22.0% (k=3) and 20.9% (k=5), and in 2024 the MA(5) (48.8%) trails the raw value (53.6%) by 4.8 points.",
   "Interpretation": "Moving averages are useful for trend communication but understate progress during acceleration and hide data-quality problems; reports should show raw and smoothed series together."
  },
  {
   "#": "F6",
   "Evidence": "Fig. 6",
   "Observation": "17 country-years are anomalous (|robust z| > 3); 8 are Zimbabwe, and most occur as drop-then-recovery pairs (e.g. Zimbabwe 2014\u201315, Malawi 2018\u201319).",
   "Interpretation": "Real one-year losses of 5\u20138 points of national access followed by recovery are implausible; the pattern is more consistent with survey-based re-estimation. Zimbabwe's economic crises could contribute but cannot be separated from measurement noise here."
  },
  {
   "#": "F7",
   "Evidence": "Fig. 7, 8, 9",
   "Observation": "Tanzania holds 29% of the region's people without electricity (32.6 M) at a mid-table rate; Malawi is worst by rate but 3rd by count; South Africa at 90% still has 6.3 M without power.",
   "Interpretation": "Rate and count rankings answer different questions: rates for judging performance, counts for sizing investment by people reached. Choosing one map alone would misdirect either evaluation or targeting."
  },
  {
   "#": "F8",
   "Evidence": "Fig. 10",
   "Observation": "The nine-country total of people without electricity rose from 103 M (2000) to a peak of 122 M (2014) and was 114 M in 2024, although every country's access rate rose.",
   "Interpretation": "Population growth absorbed connection gains for the first half of the period; universal-access progress measured in percentages overstates progress measured in people."
  }
 ]
}